y of  saying making it  positive right so we're going to do  something called sine analysis and what  you do is you just pick a point  in each of these intervals so for  example maybe negative 3 over here  if i put negative 3 in for x i get  negative 6  plus 5 is negative 1 but i'm just going  to call that negative  okay negative 3 times 2 is negative 6  minus 5 is negative 11. that's also  negative  a negative times a negative is a  positive so i'm just going to put a plus  sign there  so any point in this interval okay all  the way to negative infinity it's going  to be positive  let's pick a point in this interval how  about 0 if i put 0 in  2 times 0 is 0 plus 5 is 5. that's  positive  2 times 0 is 0 minus 5 that's negative a  positive times a negative  is a negative so i'll put a minus sign  or a negative there  if we pick a point over here to say like  positive 3 2 times 3 is 6 plus 5 is 11  that's positive  2 times 3 is 6 minus 5 is 1 that's also  positive  a positive times a positive is a  positive  so now we can see we want to know where  it's greater than or equal to zero  so it's going to be when x is less than  or equal to negative five halves  okay or now in math remember or means  union it means both x is greater than or  equal to  5 halves you're going to be to the right  of this point or to the  left of this point or it'll equal 0  right at those points now if you  know interval notation we're starting  over here at negative infinity this is  positive infinity so it's going to be  from negative infinity  all the way to five halves including  five halves so i use the square bracket  you can't reach infinity that's going to  be the parenthesis that's like an open  on this side union okay union's like or  that's going to be this  and this both these regions five halves  okay so we're using the square bracket  because it includes five halves all the  way to  positive infinity can never reach  infinity that's open okay with the  parenthesis  so interval notation or inequality  notation whichever way your teacher  prefers now you might be saying mario  you know i don't really understand what  all this is about right  and here's and i understand see the  thing is if we were to graph this in not  one dimension but in two dimensions like  on an x y  coordinate plane okay like see this is  zero here this is your origin  you would have a parabola that would  look something like  this okay roughly and so what you can  see is  it's basically saying like where is the  parabola equal to 0  that's right here at these x-intercepts  or greater than 0 meaning above the  x-axis  and that's going to be when you're to  the left here see how you're above the  x-axis over here  you're below the x-axis right but what  we're doing is just a quicker way just  analyzing it in one dimension okay on  the number line as opposed to going  through all the trouble of graphing the  whole graph and seeing where it's above  the x-axis or equal to  you know right on the x-axis let's look  at another example see if you can do  this one  so for number two we have x squared  minus x is less than 12.  okay so remember step number one get  everything on one side  and set it to zero right  okay step number two factor  as much as you can so what multiplies to  negative 12 but adds to negative one  that's negative four and positive three  you can check your work by foiling this  out  and make sure it comes out to the  original there  step number three we set each factor  to zero so we make a little mini  equation  add four to both sides subtract three x  equals negative three and then  step number what do we have step number  four we plotted on the number line from  lowest to highest so  from negative three four okay you don't  want to reverse it not make sure the  lowest number always the highest number  and you could when you factor you could  have more than uh  two solutions here you could have three  four five however many  but now in this one see how 